  faqs: [
    { q: 'Is homeschooling legal in Austria?', a: 'Yes — haeuslicher Unterricht lawfully fulfils the nine-year Schulpflicht under § 11 of the Schulpflichtgesetz, provided the education is at least equivalent to public school. The framework is annual and precise: notification to the Bildungsdirektion by the published mid-July deadline, a compulsory reflection meeting in late winter, and an external examination in the 1 June window each year.' },
    { q: 'What exactly happens if the Externistenpruefung goes wrong?', a: 'The tightest sanction in Europe, and we state it plainly: a failed, missed, or late-certified exam means school attendance is mandatorily ordered on the grade not passed, the exam cannot be retaken, administrative penalty proceedings can follow, and further home-education applications are refused. The entire Smartious Austria model — calendar planning, German preparation, mock exams — exists because of this paragraph.' },
    { q: 'What is the Reflexionsgespraech?', a: 'A compulsory pedagogical conversation at the district school around the end of the winter semester — explicitly not an exam, but missing it is a breach of Schulpflicht with school ordered by decree. It goes in the calendar with the June exam.' },
    { q: 'Are the Austrian exams in German?', a: 'Yes — the Externistenpruefung covers the grade\'s compulsory subjects in German (including Digitale Grundbildung at relevant grades). We treat German exam preparation as a first-class part of every Austrian family\'s year, alongside the English-medium Cambridge track.' },
    { q: 'Is there a free window after age 15?', a: 'No — and we will not invent one. The nine school years end around fifteen, but the AusBildung bis 18 training obligation runs to eighteen. How it treats an internationally educated teenager is a question for the coordination services or legal advice, and we refer it there.' },
    { q: 'How does Smartious compare to VIS and AIS?', a: 'Both are excellent Vienna campuses at roughly EUR 20,000-27,000 in the senior years, IB- and American-track. Smartious delivers live online Cambridge, IB, and American curriculum at USD 2,160-6,480/year — most often as the Cambridge track the city underserves, the waitlist bridge, or the rotation-proof pathway for institutional families.' },
    { q: 'Where do Austria students sit Cambridge examinations?', a: 'At authorised centres confirmed per family per session — Austria\'s CAIE provision is thin, so sittings may involve travel: the International School of Brno, a Cambridge testing centre, is under two hours from Vienna, and Munich serves the west.' },
    { q: 'Can my child access Austrian universities with A-Levels or an IB Diploma?', a: 'Yes — both are long-established credentials for international admission, subject to programme requirements, entrance procedures for restricted fields, and German proficiency for German-taught degrees. German universities next door assess both routinely, and UCAS and the Common Application remain open.' },
    { q: 'How does live class scheduling work given the Austria-Nairobi time difference?', a: 'Austria runs CET (UTC+1) in winter and CEST (UTC+2) in summer — only 1-2 hours behind Nairobi EAT. Live afternoon Nairobi teaching lands naturally within the Austrian school day.' },
    { q: 'Which Austrian cities does Smartious cover?', a: 'Vienna, Graz, Salzburg, Linz, Innsbruck/Tyrol, and Bregenz/Vorarlberg have dedicated pages with local market context. Live online delivery works identically for families anywhere in Austria — which in the Tyrol and the Rhine Valley is precisely the point.' },
  ],

